# I2P (i2pd) configuration

i2pd is a lightweight C++ I2P router. Replaces Java I2P.

## Ports
- 7070 - Web Console (http://127.0.0.1:7070 from this host; loopback only)
- 4444 - HTTP proxy (outbound)
- 4447 - SOCKS proxy (outbound)
- 7656 - SAM bridge (app access)
- 45678 - NTCP2/SSU2 transport (external, configurable)

## Addressing

An eepsite is reached at its b32 — a 52-character hash of the tunnel's
destination key, printed by `homelab i2p list`. That is the only address the
network resolves for you.

The `<service>.<home-subdomain>.i2p` host in tunnels.conf is *not* an address.
It is the Host header i2pd stamps on incoming requests (hostoverride) so Caddy
can tell services apart behind one destination. Nothing publishes it. A bare
`<service>.i2p` is worse than useless: it is a name in the global I2P namespace
that anyone can register, and a router whose addressbook holds someone else's
entry will happily send you there — which is why the home subdomain is in the
middle.

To make the name work in a browser it has to be registered.

Privately, for your own router: open the addresshelper URL from
`homelab i2p list` once through the i2p HTTP proxy.

Publicly, so anyone can resolve it: submit a signed registration line to one
registrar — reg.i2p (http://reg.i2p/add) or stats.i2p, not both. The line is
`<name>=<base64 destination>#!sign=<signature>`, signed by the destination's
own key, which is what proves the name is yours to claim.

Note the shape of our names. `searxng.leno.i2p` is a third-level domain, and
i2pd is explicit that the console generates registration lines for 2LDs only:
"result string can be used only for registering 2LD domains (example.i2p).
For registering subdomains please use i2pd-tools." So publishing this scheme
means registering the parent `leno.i2p` once, then registering each service
under it with i2pd-tools' regaddr_3ld, which signs with both keys.

## Eepsite management
Eepsite tunnels are defined in tunnels.conf.
Use the CLI:
  homelab i2p enable <service>   # add eepsite tunnel
  homelab i2p disable <service>  # remove eepsite tunnel
  homelab i2p list               # list active tunnels

After changes, i2pd is restarted automatically to pick them up. SIGHUP is
not enough: the container reads a copy of tunnels.conf taken at startup.
